## Sequenced 2027 Roadmap

The roadmap has five phases from now to December 2027. The order follows two rules in the document: secure the downside before investing (S5's short-, mid- and long-term risk order) and build foundations before applications (S8's curriculum order). The timing anchors are S2's predicted H1 2027 demand surge and the expected December–March benchmark saturation. The phase dates themselves are \[Inference\].

### Phase 0 — Stabilize and baseline (Nov–Dec 2026)

- **Topics:** security prep, worry window, TLC self-score, personal eval, one AI task a day.
- **Milestones:** resilience kit and MFA in place (S5). A 10–15-task personal eval exists (S5). Your role is scored on Trust, Learn and Contact (S4).
- **Rationale:** you can't track change without a baseline. S5 tackles short-term security risk first because it is cheap ("15 minutes"). The worry window cuts the noise that blocks action (S4).

### Phase 1 — Foundations and tooling (Jan–Feb 2027)

- **Topics:** the one-week curriculum (S8); one chat assistant plus one harness; climbing the trust ladder by giving agents boring, checkable work first (S6); moving off drag-and-drop builders (S2).
- **Milestones:** curriculum done. First reusable skills or routines built. Personal eval re-run on each new model.
- **Rationale:** S8: "I wish I had done all of this ahead of time before I learned the tools". Benchmark saturation expected between December and March makes tool fluency urgent (S2).

### Phase 2 — Judgment layer (Feb–Apr 2027)

- **Topics:** writing specs and rubrics, unknown-unknowns interviews ("grill me"), a shared domain vocabulary, reviewing only high-blast-radius code, eval design (S1, S6, S3).
- **Milestones:** a written definition of "good" for one domain or client type. An eval suite that runs each skill about 10 times with scoring (S3).
- **Rationale:** S1 bets this is where the bottleneck "stays" for 10 years. Every later phase depends on it: consulting delivery (S3 step 5), product engineering (S1) and job-proofing (S5 evals).

### Phase 3 — Go to market in the demand window (Mar–Jun 2027)

- **Topics:** RACE diagnosis → goal factoring → build → deploy → evals → team sync (S3); a packaged workshop and team-upskilling offer (S2); a distribution and sales system, 100x over technical work (S2, S5).
- **Milestones:** measurement baseline agreed with the first client. First result delivered against a guarantee. Workshop offer live. For employed engineers \[Inference\]: a product- or forward-deployed-engineer role or project secured.
- **Rationale:** S2 predicts most business owners wake up in H1 2027. Selling without Phases 1–2 means "automating broken processes" (S3).

### Phase 4 — Shift up, consolidate, hedge (Jul–Dec 2027)

- **Topics:** moving from implementation to education and embedded advisory (S2); handing over IP through skills and routines (S3); ownership and relationships (S5); downshifting where the job no longer fits (S7); short-lived, asymmetric opportunities (S2).
- **Milestones:** recurring retainer or IP-based revenue. One ownership asset or channel. Re-scored TLC and eval. A 2028 position decided.
- **Rationale:** S2 warns 2027 may be "the beginning of its decline" and admits "I don't know" beyond it. S1 expects operations and security to take 5–10 years to automate, so judgment-heavy work keeps its value longest.

## Gaps and Contradictions

The sources disagree on timelines, on job creation and on the role of specs. They also lean heavily on a single commercial voice.

**Contradictions**

- **Specs as the new code.** S1 cites Sean Grove ("code is a lossy projection of the specification") and puts the value in written specs. S6 tried spec-driven development for months, watched the code get worse, and refocused on code quality. \[Inference\] Possible reconciliation: specs as rubrics and evals (S1's actual advice) vs. specs as a replacement for code (what S6 rejected).
- **Speed of change.** S1 expects operations and security to take 5–10 years to automate ("despite people who are like AGI 2027"). S2, S5 and S8 suggest recursive self-improvement may already be under way, human-level AI within the year, and "models drop literally every 5 minutes".
- **Net jobs.** S1 expects a bigger profession in 10 years that "pays about as well". S4 says every technology destroys jobs and creates new ones. S7 calls the job-creation argument "misleading and somewhat ignorant" and expects lower-paid replacement jobs.
- **What to give agents.** S6 quotes lab leaders urging people to try their "most ambitious tasks". Pocock says the opposite: give it "your most menial boring work".
- **Harness vs. environment.** S8 says a harness matters "just as much as the actual underlying model". S6 says he "didn't care that much about the harness"; the agent's environment is what matters.
- **The consultant's role.** S3 says don't invent new systems; amplify proven ones. S2 says the future value is high-level advice on "what are you going to build in the first place".
- **Corporate path.** S6 treats promotion inside your company as a valid path. S7 has stopped chasing promotions because they "can be totally taken away".

**Gaps**

- **Source concentration and incentives.** 3 of 8 sources (S2, S3, S8) come from one speaker who pitches Maker School in each; S5 is \[Inference\] likely the same speaker. S6 carries sponsor segments. S4's "check the incentives" lens is never applied to these sources.
- **Unverified claims.** S1 says "trust me bro" and leaves citations to his slides. Incidents such as the agent swarm and the French grocery hack, and figures such as the 741% and 800%, have no traceable sources in the document.
- **No fix for training juniors.** Everyone agrees the junior ladder is gone. Only the Google APM and IBM examples are offered, with no curriculum or apprenticeship model.
- **Geography and regulation.** The data is US-only (Census, BLS-style labor graphs, Stanford). There is nothing on EU labor markets, the EU AI Act, GDPR, or data-protection duties when consultants work inside client accounts (S3).
- **Pricing.** Apart from guarantees (S3) and the "$10k → 10 cents" contrast (S2), the document doesn't cover retainer structures or how to price workshops or advisory work.
- **Non-technical, mid-career workers.** Beyond the TLC exposure score (S4), there is no concrete path for people who are neither engineers nor consultants.
- **After 2027.** S2 explicitly says "I don't know". No source gives a 2028+ scenario plan or triggers for changing course.
- **Open models and cost.** Open models get a passing mention (S6). Token costs appear only as a reason for layoffs (S7). Neither is analyzed.

## Handoff Brief for Tool Discovery

This brief lists 33 capability needs for a downstream agent to match against MCP servers, plugins, repositories and open-source utilities. Each need is tagged with its roadmap phase (P0–P4) and its source. No tools are named on purpose.

### Engineering roles

1. **Multi-reviewer code review aggregation:** run several AI reviewers on one diff and merge their non-overlapping findings, since no two reviewers found the same bugs (P2; S1).
2. **Blast-radius classification of changes:** flag high-risk code paths that need human sign-off and route the rest to automated review (P2; S1).
3. **Requirements-interview tooling:** an agent that questions the user relentlessly to surface unknown unknowns before building (P2; S6).
4. **Domain-glossary management:** keep one shared vocabulary in sync across code, database, UI and client conversations (P2; S6).
5. **Rubric and eval authoring:** turn expert judgment into machine-checkable rubrics, including turning reviewer thumbs-down votes into rules (P2; S1).
6. **Delivery metrics:** measure shipped output, the rate at which review comments get acted on, and the gap between "tests pass" and "maintainer would merge" (P2–P3; S1).
7. **Agent-operable app runtime:** let agents run, click through and screenshot complex local apps for self-verification (P1–P2; S6).
8. **Production monitoring with model-graded evals** (P3; S1).
9. **Multi-agent orchestration:** a "chief of staff" that runs long-running sub-agents and checks their plans (P3–P4; S6).
10. **Junior training environments:** simulated customer-discovery and spec-writing practice for entry-level product engineers (P4; S1).

### AI consulting

11. **Discovery-call funnel capture:** record Reach/Acquire/Convert/Expand numbers and flag the weakest stage (P3; S3).
12. **Goal-factoring tree builder:** break a goal into concrete actions and export an agent-ready action plan (P3; S3).
13. **Ad-platform integration:** pull 90-day ad performance, generate creative variants and stage them as paused ads for QA (P3; S3).
14. **Scheduled cloud execution:** run agent workflows as routines or scheduled tasks, and host procedural scripts serverlessly (P1, P3; S3).
15. **Skill packaging and org-wide distribution:** an admin pushes skills and SOPs to every team member (P3–P4; S2, S3).
16. **Eval harness for skills:** run a skill N times, collect human scores, and re-run as regression on each model release (P2–P3; S3).
17. **Chat-log mining:** extract the implicit SOPs from top performers' agent sessions and turn them into shared skills (P3; S3).
18. **Workshop and training delivery:** curricula, weekly upskilling schedules and team-capability tracking (P3–P4; S2).
19. **Client measurement and attribution:** baseline KPIs at kickoff and track results against guarantees (P3; S3).
20. **Multi-client account management:** team or enterprise workspaces with secure credential and 2FA handover (P3; S3).

### Automation trends

21. **Model-release and benchmark monitoring:** alert on new models and benchmark saturation (P0–P4; S2, S5).
22. **No-code migration:** convert drag-and-drop workflows into agent and code pipelines (P1; S2).
23. **Higher-bandwidth output generation:** video, 3D and simulation deliverables from automated pipelines (P4; S2).
24. **Opportunity scanning:** watch freelance platforms, startup request lists and market signals for short-lived openings (P4; S2, S8).
25. **Agent sandboxing and guardrails:** contain agents' network and tool access (P1–P4; S4, S5).

### Survival strategies

26. **Personal eval runner:** store 10–15 real tasks with their context files and score each new model against them (P0; S5).
27. **Batched news intake:** collect AI news for a weekly window and tag each item by source camp and incentive (P0; S4).
28. **Offline knowledge kit:** a local LLM plus an offline encyclopedia (P0; S5).
29. **Account-security audit:** find SMS-based 2FA and migrate it to authenticator-based MFA (P0; S5).
30. **Job-exposure self-assessment:** score a role on Trust, Learn and Contact (P0, P4; S4).
31. **Relationship and network management:** maintain personal and professional ties (P4; S5).

### Career roadmaps for 2027

32. **Learning-path tracker:** sequence and track progress through the S8 curriculum resources, with primary-source "teach me" sessions that quote and link sources (P1; S6, S8).
33. **Personal brand and distribution pipeline:** content drafting, publishing and audience analytics, plus a lightweight solo sales CRM and follow-up automation (P3–P4; S2, S5, S7).
